(begin-tx "ATS-G5 — ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART: four sequential guards, four distinct trips")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [ATS-G5 · 01 · kickstart admission] ---")
;;Kickstarting sets an ATS pair's opening index — the exchange rate between reward tokens and the
;;reward-bearing token. Get it wrong and every later stake in that pool is mispriced, so the cap
;;checks four things in a deliberate order. The source says why: caller-input checks run FIRST,
;;pool-state checks LAST, "lets bound violations be rejected before ever touching state".
;;
;;   1  (= l1 l2)                    rt-amounts must have one entry per reward token
;;   2  (> rbt-request-amount 0.0)   you cannot ask for zero RBT
;;   3  (>= would-be-index 0.1)      the resulting index must clear the floor
;;   4  (= index -1.0)               ...and the pair must not already be kickstarted
;;
;;Each masks the ones after it, so all four inputs below are built to PASS their predecessors.
;;Case 3 is the one that needs thought: the amounts are valid and the request is positive, but
;;asking for 1000 RBT against 1 unit of each reward token drives the computed index below the
;;0.1 floor. A first draft that simply passed bad numbers would have landed on guard 1.
;;
;;THE ARITY IS DERIVED, NOT GUESSED. My first attempt hardcoded a one-element list and all four
;;cases tripped guard 1 — the pair has a different number of reward tokens, so nothing past the
;;first guard was ever reached. Reading the length from the pair makes the block correct whatever
;;the fixture holds, and the bad case is that length PLUS ONE rather than an arbitrary size.
;;==== ATS-G5 — ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART: four sequential guards, four distinct trips · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [ATS-G5 — ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART: four sequential guards, four distinct trips · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let*
    (
        (ref-ATS:module{AutostakeV3} ATS)
        (ps:string "PlebeicStrength-98c486052a51")
        (n:integer (length (ref-ATS::UR_RewardTokenList ps)))
        (ok:[decimal] (make-list n 1.0))
        (too-many:[decimal] (make-list (+ n 1) 1.0))
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;precondition: this pair is ALREADY kickstarted, which is what makes guard 4
            ;;reachable at all. Asserted rather than assumed.
            (expect "<<ATS-G5>> the pair is already kickstarted (index is not -1.0)"
                false (= (ref-ATS::URC_Index ps) -1.0))
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G5>> one amount too many for the pair's reward-token count"
                "RT-Amounts list does not correspond with the Number of the ATS-Pair Reward Tokens"
                (test-capability (ATSU.ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART KST.ANHD ps too-many 1.0)))
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G5>> ...correct arity, but a zero RBT request"
                "RBT Request Amount must be greater than zero!"
                (test-capability (ATSU.ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART KST.ANHD ps ok 0.0)))
            ;;valid arity, positive request -- and still refused, because the RATIO it implies
            ;;puts the opening index under the floor.
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G5>> ...and a request so large the resulting index falls under 0.1"
                "KickStart index must be at least 0.1"
                (test-capability (ATSU.ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART KST.ANHD ps ok 1000.0)))
            ;;everything a fresh pair would need is now valid, so the ONLY remaining objection is
            ;;that this pair has already been kickstarted -- which is guard 4 and nothing else.
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G5>> ...and finally, a wholly valid request on an ALREADY-kickstarted pair"
                "Kickstarting can only be done on ATS-Pairs with -1 Index"
                (test-capability (ATSU.ATSU|C>X_KICKSTART KST.ANHD ps ok 1.0)))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
(commit-tx)

