(begin-tx "ATS-G11 -- VestedCoil: whose signature is actually required")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== ATS-G11 -- VestedCoil: whose signature is actually required · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== ====
(print "--- [ATS-G11 -- VestedCoil: whose signature is actually required · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_Emma", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [ATS-G11 . 01 . a third party cannot coil someone else's tokens] ---")
;;FOUND BY `python3 tools/_docclaims.py`, auditing @doc text for AUTHORITY claims no test pins.
;;TS01-C2's ATS|C_VestedCoil says "Only the Owner of <coil-token> can execute this function".
;;
;;THE DOC'S WORDING IS MISLEADING, and that is worth recording rather than quietly testing around.
;;"Owner of <coil-token>" reads as the token's REGISTRAR-owner -- the account in DPTF's owner-konto
;;field. It cannot mean that: Stage_01/[6.7]_VST.repl:142 coils OURO as ANHD, and ANHD does NOT own
;;OURO (checked -- OURO's owner-konto is neither ANHD nor EMMA). That call is correct and passes.
;;So "Owner" here means the HOLDER: the <coiler-vester> whose balance is being coiled.
;;
;;The security-relevant property -- the one actually enforced -- is the stronger reading: a third
;;party cannot cause someone ELSE's tokens to be coiled and vested away. EMMA names ANHD as the
;;coiler-vester and signs only for herself; the rejection demands ANHD's keyset, which is the
;;account-ownership gate speaking.
;;
;;EliteAuryndex is used rather than Auryndex because by this point in the file Auryndex has
;;HIBERNATION on, and that check fires first -- the test would then pass on the wrong error and
;;prove nothing about authority.
(map print
    [
        (expect-failure "<<ATS-G11>> a third party cannot coil and vest another account's tokens"
            "Keyset failure (keys-all)"
            (TS01-C2.ATS|C_VestedCoil KST.EMMA KST.ANHD "EliteAuryndex-98c486052a51"
                "AURYN-98c486052a51" 100.0 KST.EMMA 3600 7200 5))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

