(begin-tx "ATS-G14 — the ledger-permission gate, and the Hot-RBT type gate")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 150000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [ATS-G14 · 01 · who may touch another account's ATS ledger] ---")
;;ATSU|C>NORMALIZE_LEDGER is an enforce-one over FIVE guards: DALOS admin, ATSU admin, the account's
;;own guard, its sovereign's guard, or its governor. It sits under ATSU|C>DEPLOY, so every client that
;;deploys a ledger row for an account passes through it.
;;
;;WHY IT LOOKED UNREACHABLE, and the trap worth writing down: the first attempt signed
;;PK_AncientHodler -- which IS the DALOS admin keyset -- so the FIRST branch of the enforce-one
;;succeeded and the call sailed through to fail later on EMMA's ownership with a bare
;;"Keyset failure (keys-all): [PK_Emma…]". That looks like the guard firing and is not. Reaching an
;;enforce-one's own message means failing EVERY branch, so the caller must hold NONE of the five --
;;here LUMY, a funded third party who is neither admin nor related to EMMA.
;;==== ATS-G14 — the ledger-permission gate, and the Hot-RBT type gate · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [ATS-G14 — the ledger-permission gate, and the Hot-RBT type gate · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_Lumy", "caps": [] }])
(print
    (expect-failure "<<ATS-G14>> a third party with no admin and no relation to the account is refused"
        "Invalid permission for normalizing ATS|Ledger Account Operations"
        (TS01-C2.ATS|C_ColdRecovery KST.LUMY KST.EMMA "Auryndex-98c486052a51" 1.0)))
(print "--- [ATS-G14 · 02 · FIXED: both 'Invalid Hot-RBT' guards now speak] ---")
;;ATSU|C>REDEEM (10_ATSU.pact:521) and ATS|C>RECOVER (:508) carry the same message, "Invalid Hot-RBT",
;;on the same check `(enforce iz-rbt …)` where `iz-rbt` is `DPOF::URC_IzRBT id`. NEITHER can ever be
;;shown. Pinned AS IT BEHAVES.
;;
;;WHY. Both defuns bind the guard's own subject ABOVE the `with-capability`, in an EAGER `let`:
;;
;;    C_Redeem  (:1973)  (ats:string (ref-DPOF::UR_RewardBearingToken id))
;;                       (rt-lst:[string] (ref-ATS::UR_RewardTokenList ats))     <-- same let group
;;    C_Recover (:1928)  (ats:string (ref-DPOF::UR_RewardBearingToken id))
;;                       (c-rbt:string (ref-ATS::UR_ColdRewardBearingToken ats)) <-- same let group
;;
;;For a token that is NOT reward-bearing, `UR_RewardBearingToken` returns the BAR sentinel, so the
;;very next binding looks up ATS pair `|` and dies there -- before the capability is acquired and
;;therefore before the enforce that exists to explain exactly this. The caller gets
;;`No value found in table ouronet-ns.ATS_ATS|Pairs for key: |`.
;;
;;This is the SIXTH instance of one pattern in this codebase -- the guard's subject bound above it in
;;an eager let. The others: OUROBOROS::UEV_Exchange (two dead guards, pinned at
;;[4.0] <<TX4.0-CONFIG>>), PYTHIA, FVT, DALOS::GAS_PAYER, and the SCORE triplet. The fix shape is the
;;same every time: bind the dependent value lazily inside the body, or hoist the sentinel check above
;;the let. DPTF::URCv_Parent is the in-repo example of the correct order (pinned by DPTF-G5).
;;
;;FIXED 2026-09-12, owner-ruled. Both defuns now acquire their capability BEFORE the `let`, so the
;;cap's own enforce answers first. That was the minimal form of the "hoist" option: both caps take
;;only plain defun parameters, so nothing in the binding group was needed to build them -- and it is
;;the shape StoicSyntax asks for anyway (validation in the defcap, work in the body). The reader-side
;;alternative was rejected here: `UR_ColdRewardBearingToken` and `UR_RewardTokenList` have 22 and 28
;;call sites, so defaulting them would change what fifty places see when a pair is missing. Where the
;;abort is a MISSING ROW the reader fix is right (00_Demipad); where it is a BAR SENTINEL lookup, the
;;call-site fix is.
;;
;;The input below is chosen to isolate the cause: `H|AURYN-98c486052a51` nonce 1 is a REAL
;;hibernation-token nonce on this chain, so neither the token nor the nonce is missing. The only
;;thing wrong with it is that it is not reward-bearing -- which is precisely what the mute guard was
;;written to say.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
;;==== ATS-G14 — the ledger-permission gate, and the Hot-RBT type gate · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [ATS-G14 — the ledger-permission gate, and the Hot-RBT type gate · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let ((h:string "H|AURYN-98c486052a51"))
    (map print
        [
            (expect "<<ATS-G14>> H|AURYN exists with nonce 1 and is NOT a reward-bearing token"
                [false true]
                [(DPOF.URC_IzRBT h) (!= -1.0 (DPOF.UR_NonceSupply h 1))])
            (expect "<<ATS-G14>> ...and its reward-bearing-token field is the BAR sentinel"
                (U|CT.CT_BAR) (DPOF.UR_RewardBearingToken h))
            ;;REDEEM now answers with its OWN guard instead of dying on the BAR pair lookup.
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G14>> Redeem refuses a non-reward-bearing token BY NAME"
                "Invalid Hot-RBT"
                (TS01-C2.ATS|C_Redeem KST.ANHD KST.ANHD h 1))
            ;;...and so does RECOVER, which carried the identical mute guard and the identical cause.
            ;;Both are driven, because they are two separate defuns that had to be fixed separately --
            ;;fixing C_Recover alone left this test green, which is how the second one was found.
            ;;(the Talos wrapper for ATSU::C_Recover is named ATS|C_Reverse, not ATS|C_Recover)
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G14>> ...and so does Recover, the twin that had the same defect"
                "Invalid Hot-RBT"
                (TS01-C2.ATS|C_Reverse KST.ANHD KST.ANHD h 1))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(commit-tx)

