(begin-tx "ATS-G23 — the hot-RBT re-registration route, driven to the wall it actually hits")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [ATS-G23 · 01 · registering an already-registered DPOF as a second pair's hot-RBT] ---")
;;THIS BLOCK EXISTS TO SETTLE A GUARD, AND IT SETTLES IT AS UNREACHABLE. `06_DPOF.pact:2617` reads
;;"RBT-Data for DPOF {} is already set as ATS-Pair {}" and refuses a DPOF that is already some
;;pair's hot reward-bearing token. The obvious route is to take a registered hot-RBT and offer it to
;;a second pair. Everything that route needs exists here — THRBT is TestKickPair's hot-RBT, its
;;supply is 0.0 so the zero-supply rule admits it, and G23a opened KORIndex's last gate.
;;
;;IT STILL CANNOT BE REACHED, and the reason is the eager `let` in its only caller. ATS::C_AddHotRBT
;;binds <ico2> to `DPOF::C_Control hot-rbt false false false false true true false false`, whose
;;FIRST argument is can-upgrade := false. Pact evaluates let bindings before the body, so the first
;;successful registration latches can-upgrade off, and C_Control needs can-upgrade true to run at
;;all — the latch is one-way. The second attempt therefore dies one binding ABOVE the DPOF guard,
;;with the can-upgrade message. Asserted below rather than argued: the message that comes back is
;;read, and it is not the RBT one.
;;
;;NOT FILED AS A DEFECT. The operation is correctly refused either way; only the wording is less
;;specific than it could be. Reordering writes inside a Stage-1 client path to improve a message is
;;a worse trade than documenting it, so `06_DPOF:2617` carries a `;;UNREACHABLE` annotation pointing
;;back here. If ico2 ever stops clearing can-upgrade, this test fails and the guard becomes live.
;;==== ATS-G23 — the hot-RBT re-registration route, driven to the wall it actually hits · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [ATS-G23 — the hot-RBT re-registration route, driven to the wall it actually hits · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
;;==== ATS-G23 — the hot-RBT re-registration route, driven to the wall it actually hits · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [ATS-G23 — the hot-RBT re-registration route, driven to the wall it actually hits · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-C2:module{TalosStageOne_ClientTwoV2} TS01-C2)
        (ref-ATS:module{AutostakeV3} ATS)
        (ref-DPOF:module{DemiourgosPactOrtoFungibleV2} DPOF)
        (kor:string "KORIndex-98c486052a51")
        (thrbt:string "THRBT-98c486052a51")
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;every precondition the route needs, read live -- this is the part that shows the
            ;;fixture is NOT the blocker
            (expect "<<ATS-G23>> THRBT is already a pair's hot-RBT, at zero supply"
                ["TestKickPair-98c486052a51" 0.0]
                [(ref-DPOF::UR_RewardBearingToken thrbt) (ref-DPOF::UR_Supply thrbt)])
            (expect "<<ATS-G23>> KORIndex is a DIFFERENT pair, with no hot-RBT and both recoveries off"
                [false false]
                [(ref-ATS::UR_ToggleColdRecovery kor) (ref-ATS::UR_ToggleDirectRecovery kor)])
            ;;THE LATCH, and the reason the route dead-ends: the first registration turned this off
            ;;and C_Control can never turn it back on, because C_Control itself requires it true.
            (expect "<<ATS-G23>> ...but THRBT's can-upgrade flag was latched off by its FIRST registration"
                false (ref-DPOF::UR_CanUpgrade thrbt))
            ;;so the attempt is refused ONE BINDING ABOVE the DPOF guard. The message proves which
            ;;rule answered.
            (expect-failure "<<ATS-G23>> so re-registering it is refused by the can-upgrade latch, NOT the RBT rule"
                "Orto-Fungible THRBT-98c486052a51 properties cannot be upgraded"
                (ref-TS01-C2::ATS|C_AddHotRBT KST.ANHD kor thrbt))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(commit-tx)

